   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 357 of the laws of 2017 amending the education law and\nthe public health law relating to the substitution of interchangeable\nbiological products for prescribed products, in relation to making such\nprovisions permanent",
    "basePrintNo": "S8465",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8465-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §5, Chap 357 of 2017",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8465\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 357 of the laws of 2017 amending the education\nlaw and the public health law relating to the substitution of inter-\nchangeable biological products for prescribed products, in relation to\nmaking such provisions permanent\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo make permanent existing law that allows pharmacists to substitute\nbiosimilar medications under certain conditions.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill amends the enacting date of the original statute to strike the\nsunset of that law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nBiologic therapies have revolutionized the treatment of complex chronic\ndiseases such as cancer, heart disease, arthritis, multiple sclerosis,\nHIV/AIDS, among others. There are currently more than 200 FDA approved\nbiologic medications. Like the generic drugs that pharmacists may\nsubstitute for their branded counterparts, the federal Food and Drug\nAdministration approves biologic medications that are interchangeable,\nreferred to as \"biosimilars\". Because the interchangeable biosimilar is\nnot an identical molecule as is the case with generic drugs, a separate\nlaw was required to authorize the substitution.\n\nThe current New York law permitting substitution with FDA-approved bios-\nimilar products is similar to the law authorizing substitution of gener-\nic drugs for brand-name drugs.\n\nThe biosimilar law will sunset in October 2022. This bill would make the\nexisting law permanent. This bill will ensure that patients with diffi-\ncult to treat and rare conditions will continue to benefit from the\navailability of biologic medications that are designated as interchange-\nable by the FDA.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
